            <ArticleTitle>Hereditary leiomyomatosis and renal cell cancer syndrome: a family affair.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr2016218270</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2016-218270</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 49-year-old woman with cutaneous and uterine leiomyomas, flank pain and a family history of hereditary leiomyomatosis and renal cell cancer (HLRCC) syndrome sought genetic testing. She was found to harbour a fumarate hydratase (FH) genetic mutation and a previously undetected renal tumour. The patient underwent radical nephrectomy, and remains well at follow-up. HLRCC syndrome is a rare autosomal dominant disease, with patients at increased risk for cutaneous leiomyomas, early-onset uterine leiomyomas and aggressive renal carcinoma. Although the syndrome may manifest life-threatening complications, outcomes may be improved by preventative family screening and surveillance, compelling early diagnosis.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>2017 BMJ Publishing Group Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
